A config class attempts to use one member to initialize another, but engineers see sporadic garbage in logs on startup:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
struct Config {
    int port;      // declared first
    int effective; // declared second
    Config() : effective(port), port(8080) {
        std::cout << effective << "\n";
    }
};
```
On some runs, the program prints unpredictable numbers instead of 8080.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Members are initialized in the order of their declaration, regardless of the order in the initializer list. Here, `port` is indeterminate when `effective(port)` runs, so `effective` is initialized from garbage; later `port` is set to 8080. Reading that indeterminate value is undefined behavior.

Corrected code:
```cpp
struct Config {
    int port;
    int effective;
    Config() : port(8080), effective(port) {}
};
```
Rule: Initialization follows declaration order in the class definition; the initializer list order is ignored.

scenario_questions.scenario:
A plugin system deletes derived objects via base pointers and sometimes leaks resources. The base class is also used polymorphically elsewhere:

```cpp
struct Plugin {
    virtual void run() = 0;
    ~Plugin() {} // current code
};
struct ZipPlugin : Plugin {
    ~ZipPlugin() { /* releases zip handles, logs */ }
    void run() override { /*...*/ }
};

void unload(Plugin* p) {
    delete p; // occasionally only base dtor seems to run
}
```
QA noticed logs from ZipPlugin's destructor are missing in some builds.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Deleting through a base pointer requires a virtual destructor to ensure the most-derived destructor runs. Here, Plugin's destructor is non-virtual, so `delete p;` has undefined behavior; often only the base destructor runs, leaking ZipPlugin resources.

Minimal fix:
```cpp
struct Plugin {
    virtual ~Plugin() = default; // make it virtual
    virtual void run() = 0;
};
```
Now `delete p;` dispatches to `ZipPlugin::~ZipPlugin()` first, then `Plugin::~Plugin()`. Whether Plugin is abstract is irrelevant to this requirement; abstract bases still need virtual destructors if deleted via base pointers.

