scenario_questions.scenario:
You inherit a cleanup routine that crashes intermittently:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <memory>

struct Resource { bool expired() const; void close(); };

void cleanup(std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Resource>>& rs) {
    for (auto it = rs.begin(); it != rs.end(); ++it) {
        if ((*it)->expired()) {
            // attempt to be "safe"
            (*it)->close();          // A: manual shutdown
            delete it->get();        // B: manual delete
            rs.erase(it);            // C: erase from container
        }
    }
}
```
In production this sometimes double-frees or crashes, and sometimes leaks if exceptions are thrown inside `close()`.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Issues:
- Calling `delete it->get()` violates `unique_ptr`'s sole ownership contract and causes double-delete; the `unique_ptr`'s destructor will also delete on scope exit or erase.
- Erasing from `vector` invalidates iterators at and after the point of erase; the loop uses `++it` unconditionally, so after `erase(it)` the iterator is invalid and then incremented (undefined behavior) or the next element is skipped if the code were adjusted improperly.
- If `close()` throws, the function may leak by skipping `erase`, leaving an expired resource in the vector, and may crash later; manual deletion before `erase` makes exception windows worse.

Fix:
- Do not manually `delete`. Let `unique_ptr` own deletion.
- Use the erase-return iterator pattern to maintain a valid iterator after erase.
- Make shutdown idempotent or non-throwing; if `close()` can throw, catch locally and proceed to erase to maintain container invariants.

Example corrected code:

```cpp
void cleanup(std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Resource>>& rs) {
    for (auto it = rs.begin(); it != rs.end(); /* no ++ here */) {
        if ((*it)->expired()) {
            try { (*it)->close(); } catch (...) { /* log and proceed */ }
            it = rs.erase(it); // returns the next valid iterator
        } else {
            ++it;
        }
    }
}
```
Justification:
- RAII: `unique_ptr`'s destructor handles deletion; never call `delete` on its `get()`.
- Iterator validity: `vector::erase` invalidates iterators at/after the point; assigning the return value preserves a valid iterator to continue.
- Exception safety: catching around `close()` prevents partially cleaned items remaining; container remains in a valid state (basic guarantee) even if `close()` throws.

scenario_questions.scenario:
A team models a parent-child hierarchy with shared ownership, then observes memory never drops after releasing top-level references:

```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <string>

struct Node {
    std::string name;
    std::shared_ptr<Node> parent;
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Node>> children;
};

std::map<std::string, std::shared_ptr<Node>> index;
```
Each child has `parent` set to its parent; parents keep `children`. After building a tree and clearing `index`, the process memory remains high.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Root cause: `shared_ptr` cycles (parent <-> children) prevent reference counts from reaching zero; clearing `index` drops one set of owners, but cycles keep objects alive.

Change: Use `std::weak_ptr<Node>` for the `parent` backlink because parent lifetime should not be extended by its children. Downward traversal retains ownership (`shared_ptr` in `children`); upward traversal locks the `weak_ptr` to temporarily access the parent.

Containers: `std::map` and `std::vector` store `shared_ptr`/`weak_ptr` by value and manage counts non-intrusively. `map` iterators remain valid except for erased elements; `vector` reallocation invalidates iterators if you push_back beyond capacity, but pointer semantics (shared/weak) are independent of container iterator stability.

Fragments:

```cpp
struct Node {
    std::string name;
    std::weak_ptr<Node> parent;                   // weak back-link
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Node>> children;  // strong down-links
};

// Upward traversal
if (auto p = child->parent.lock()) {
    // use p safely
}
```
This breaks cycles while keeping traversal functionality. Clearing `index` will then allow nodes to be destroyed when no other strong owners remain.

