scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Prefer B with a slight refinement:
- Use `std::map<std::string, std::shared_ptr<Resource>>` in the registry to provide key-sorted iteration inherently and stable iterators that are not invalidated by other insertions/erasures (except for the erased element itself).
- Clients should hold `std::weak_ptr<Resource>` handles, so they can observe lifetime without extending it. They convert to `shared_ptr` temporarily when using the resource via `lock()`.
- This ensures that removing an entry from the registry invalidates only that entry; other client handles remain valid (they may lock to null). Multiple clients can safely access the same underlying object concurrently when they upgrade their `weak_ptr`.

Illustration:

```cpp
struct Handle { std::weak_ptr<Resource> wp; };

class Registry {
    std::map<std::string, std::shared_ptr<Resource>> reg_;
public:
    Handle getHandle(const std::string& k) const {
        auto it = reg_.find(k);
        return Handle{ it == reg_.end() ? std::weak_ptr<Resource>{} : std::weak_ptr<Resource>(it->second) };
    }
    void insert(const std::string& k, std::shared_ptr<Resource> r) { reg_[k] = std::move(r); }
    void erase(const std::string& k) { reg_.erase(k); }

    // iteration in sorted key order is natural for map
    auto begin() const { return reg_.begin(); }
    auto end() const { return reg_.end(); }
};

// Client use
void use(Handle h){ if (auto sp = h.wp.lock()) { sp->doWork(); } }
```
Rationale vs requirements:
- Sorted iteration: `map` maintains order by key.
- Handles that don't extend lifetime: `weak_ptr`.
- Shared concurrent references: `shared_ptr` on upgrade; thread-safety of the object itself is separate.
- Iterator/handle stability: Inserting/erasing other keys does not invalidate iterators or existing `weak_ptr`s; only erased entries become non-lockable, which is exactly the desired signal.

