scenario_questions.scenario:
A test intermittently crashes when sorting views of lines from a file:

```cpp
std::vector<std::string_view> views;
std::string line;
while (std::getline(in, line)) {
    views.push_back(std::string_view{line}); // later, sort(views.begin(), views.end())
}
```
Sometimes the sort dereferences dangling views and UB ensues. Locally it's fine; under ASan it reproduces.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Diagnosis: `std::string_view` refers to `line`'s internal buffer. Each `getline` may reallocate `line`, invalidating all previously stored views. After the loop, `views` hold pointers to memory that may have moved or been overwritten. Sorting dereferences dangling pointers, causing UB.

Fix 1 (own the data): store `std::string` in a vector, not views:
```cpp
std::vector<std::string> lines;
std::string line;
while (std::getline(in, line)) lines.push_back(line);
std::sort(lines.begin(), lines.end());
```
Test: unit test reads a small file with varying line lengths to force reallocations and asserts sorted order. Run under ASan to catch regressions.

Fix 2 (stable backing storage for views): append to a `std::deque<std::string>` whose elements don't relocate on push_back, then take views:
```cpp
std::deque<std::string> store;
std::vector<std::string_view> views;
for (std::string line; std::getline(in, line); ) {
  store.push_back(line);
  views.push_back(store.back());
}
std::sort(views.begin(), views.end());
```
Test: same as above; also add a test that shuffles `views` and compares to a separately sorted copy of `store` to ensure view validity.

Both fixes remove dependency on a mutating buffer's address, eliminating the UB and making the code robust across platforms and optimization levels.
